WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.399 --> 00:00:08.559
This video is my entry for Project for Awesome,
an annual charity event on Youtube where we

00:00:08.559 --> 00:00:12.719
raise money and vote for our favourite nonprofits
to give that money to.

00:00:12.719 --> 00:00:16.789
I’ll reveal which organisation I’m supporting
in a few minutes.

00:00:16.789 --> 00:00:23.539
First though, let me tell you a story about
how powerful the scientific method can be.

00:00:23.539 --> 00:00:28.590
In the late 90s, the Harvard professor Michael
Kremer, was interested in improving education

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:30.169
in Kenya.

00:00:30.169 --> 00:00:34.500
Instead of starting an education nonprofit,
like you might expect, he decided to do a

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.360
series of Randomised Control Trials.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:41.630
This is what they entailed: he randomly split
up the schools he was studying in Kenya into

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:42.950
two groups.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:46.870
One group would continue with their schooling
as usual, and the other group would receive

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:50.990
the new interventions like more textbooks
and more teachers.

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:55.640
Finally he’d compare the outcomes of the
students, and, provided the sample was big

00:00:55.640 --> 00:01:01.930
enough, could conclude that any differences
between the groups were due to the intervention.

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:06.250
As you probably know, this sort of Randomised
control trial is exactly how we test if a

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:10.700
medicine is effective, but up until this point
they were unheard of in the not for profit

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:11.700
sector.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:16.200
For obvious reasons, of course this intervention
is going to be effective- why should someone

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.630
waste precious resources testing that?

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:24.330
Well Kremer’s idea was not to test all the
different interventions at once, but to test

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:29.680
them all in separate trials, to figure which
was the most effective at improving the outcomes

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:30.680
for the students.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:35.980
To his surprise, one of these factors worked
ridiculously well.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:40.710
First they tested giving out more textbooks,
then providing flipcharts for the teachers

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:44.420
use, and then improving the teacher to student
ratio.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:48.960
Before I tell you the results, I want you
to take 10 seconds to guess which of these

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.140
you think was most effective.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:56.010
Which one would you have funded, if you had
to choose?

00:01:56.010 --> 00:02:02.930
Ok, let’s go through them.

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:08.069
Surprisingly, the extra textbooks seemed to
have no real effect.

00:02:08.069 --> 00:02:13.030
A possible explanation is that these textbooks
were too high level for the student anyway,

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:17.450
especially since they were written in English,
the student’s third language.

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:22.319
So then they tried the flipcharts, reasoning
that the teachers could tailor the lesson

00:02:22.319 --> 00:02:24.299
to the students ability this way.

00:02:24.299 --> 00:02:30.060
Again though, for whatever reason, this had
no discernible effect in the sample they trialled.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:35.329
Then they tried simply having more teachers,
but again, there was no effect.

00:02:35.329 --> 00:02:41.159
Frustrated that none of these had worked,
Kremer decided to try a crazy suggestion he’d

00:02:41.159 --> 00:02:42.159
received.

00:02:42.159 --> 00:02:48.360
His team gave the children medicine in school
that prevented them from getting stomach worms.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:51.829
Stomach worms are something many of us aren’t
familiar with, because they’re fairly rare

00:02:51.829 --> 00:02:53.499
in developed countries.

00:02:53.499 --> 00:02:57.239
But they’re devastating to a person’s
health, and far too common in some developing

00:02:57.239 --> 00:02:58.849
countries.

00:02:58.849 --> 00:03:02.779
It turned out, many children in Kenya at the
time were missing school because they were

00:03:02.779 --> 00:03:05.370
sick with worms.

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:10.129
What they found in the trial was that students
who’d got the treatment missed school far

00:03:10.129 --> 00:03:12.450
less, and as a result did far better.

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:17.549
In fact, when researchers followed up with
the students a whole 10 years later, those

00:03:17.549 --> 00:03:21.959
who’d been treated were earning 20% more
than those who hadn’t been.

00:03:21.959 --> 00:03:28.341
For just 50c per a child per a year, this
intervention had had a massive impact on their

00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:29.341
lives.

00:03:29.341 --> 00:03:36.819
The scientific method tells us that we should
make hypothesis about the world but then we

00:03:36.819 --> 00:03:38.480
need to test them.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:42.459
If they don’t hold up, no matter how much
it seems like it should be true, we need to

00:03:42.459 --> 00:03:45.219
abandon the theory and start the process again.

00:03:45.219 --> 00:03:49.579
And in this case you can see how powerful
this process can be.

00:03:49.579 --> 00:03:54.230
Some nonprofit interventions really are a
lot more effective than others, so it’s

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:58.139
worth doing this sort of rigorous analysis
to find them.

00:03:58.139 --> 00:04:04.650
To put this into perspective, here’s some
really mind bending data.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:09.809
This study compared 108 health interventions
in developing countries.

00:04:09.809 --> 00:04:14.480
To measure how successful a health intervention
is, you want to look at how many extra healthy

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:16.669
years of life it gives someone.

00:04:16.669 --> 00:04:21.690
1 extra year of health is what we’ll call
a DALY.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:27.520
This study looked at how many DALYs $1000
spent on an intervention could buy.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:34.229
What they found was, half the 108 interventions
saved people 5 years or less of life for that

00:04:34.229 --> 00:04:35.419
cost.

00:04:35.419 --> 00:04:39.810
In other words, 5 years was the median value
in the study.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:45.120
So you might expect the best interventions
save around 10-15 years.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:47.060
That’s what I would have guessed.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:51.741
But actually, a whole bunch of the remaining
half fall between 5 years all the way up to

00:04:51.741 --> 00:04:54.099
100, which is great.

00:04:54.099 --> 00:05:01.379
But amazingly, there are 5 interventions out
of the 108 that do even better.

00:05:01.379 --> 00:05:07.990
The best one saved 300 years for every $1000
spent, A massive bargain, compared to the

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:10.000
median of just 5 years.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:15.750
The majority of these intervention work just
ok, some a few of these interventions work

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:17.520
amazingly well.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:20.490
So doesn’t it make sense to find these and
fund them?

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:25.060
That’s where GiveWell, the not for profit
I’m supporting, comes in.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:30.090
They do extensive research on these sorts
of interventions, and if you donate to them,

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:32.800
they give that money to their top rated initiatives.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:38.479
At the moment that includes several organisations
focussed on deworming treatments.

00:05:38.479 --> 00:05:42.919
But GiveWell apply the scientific mindset
that we should always be updating our beliefs

00:05:42.919 --> 00:05:44.720
based on the best new evidence.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:50.669
That’s why they reassess their choices for
top non-profits every year, and are very transparent

00:05:50.669 --> 00:05:53.259
about how they come to their conclusions.

00:05:53.259 --> 00:05:57.080
If you want to support this sort of evidence
based giving, there’s two simple things

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:01.560
you can do: one, vote for them on the project
for awesome website.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:04.229
The link is in the description.

00:06:04.229 --> 00:06:08.669
Share this video, because a lot of people
have the idea that charity is useless and

00:06:08.669 --> 00:06:09.669
corrupt.

00:06:09.669 --> 00:06:14.180
I hope this video convinced you that it certainly
doesn’t have to be this way.

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:17.070
Charity can be an effective way to help those
most in need.

